# The crypt IN THE PODIUM, which is the only way its head height can be measured. NOT part of the
# building.
#
# `--gap y@x,z` walks a column of the sampled clip and reports the air in it. Run against
# crypt-probe.clip it reports the whole 21 m of the box, because in that probe there is nothing
# over the room and nothing under it: the ceiling of this room is the podium, and the floor is the
# bottom of the podium. So the head height has to be measured on the podium with the room cut out
# of it, which is this file — podium and crypt unioned, and void_crypt taken away, exactly as the
# manifest does it.
#
#   ./build/clipcheck clips/facility/requests/crypt-place.clip --metre 16 --gap y@0,0
#   ./build/clipcheck clips/facility/requests/crypt-place.clip --metre 16 --slice x@0.0

include "../_contract.clip"
include "../_order.clip"

paint limestone

include "../podium.clip"
include "../crypt.clip"

let crypt_place = difference { union { part_podium part_crypt } void_crypt }

# THE BOX IS CUT TO THE ROOM, AND ITS TOP AND BOTTOM ARE INSIDE STONE ON PURPOSE.
#
# Two reasons. The first is size: the contract's box is 582 million cells at metre 32, nearly all
# of it the air over a building this fragment is 2.10 m underneath.
#
# The second is what `--gap` actually measures. It reports the air it finds in a column, and it
# says BROKEN when that air is in more than one piece — so a box with sky in it reports the sky,
# the room and the space under the podium as one broken 3.1 m span and tells you nothing. -0.49 is
# inside the 0.05 m floor slab and 1.79 is inside the 0.15 m ceiling, so every column of this box
# begins and ends in stone and the one air run in it IS the head height. That is why the number
# this probe prints can be read straight off.
bounds -12.3 -0.49 -9.4   12.3 1.79 6.4

solid crypt_place
